MT6580 private ARM32 decoder buffer preparation
===============================================

This is a privileged bring-up tool, not an Android media HAL. Do not install
its interposer or legacy dependencies in /system or /vendor. The stock
libraries must come from the user's own firmware; none are redistributed.

Build with an Android ARM32 NDK compiler, for example:

  make CC=/absolute/path/to/armv7a-linux-androideabi28-clang

Enable CONFIG_MT6580_VCODEC and the board's M4U/LARB1 codec node. The native
/dev/mt6580-vcodec interface requires device-node permission plus CAP_SYS_RAWIO. buffer-check
uses a real system DMA heap import and a coherent allocation. It checks CPU
cache API calls, owned handles, forked mappings and memory survival after
RELEASE/close; it does not submit a decoder DMA job.

For stock-create-check, copy only the required stock driver/dependencies and
coherent legacy C++ runtime into a private /data/local/tmp directory. The
loader must resolve them through LD_LIBRARY_PATH; preload the built bridge:

  LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/data/local/tmp/private-codec/lib \
  LD_PRELOAD=/data/local/tmp/private-codec/lib/libval-buffer-bridge.so \
  /data/local/tmp/private-codec/stock-create-check \
  /data/local/tmp/private-codec/lib/libvcodecdrv.so

The bridge implements the recovered 96-byte VAL memory descriptor: normal
memory uses system DMA-BUF plus an owned real M4U IOVA; type3 uses coherent
DMA plus opaque mmap tokens. Only the low word of input ARM32 unions is
consumed, matching stock callers. Alignment up to 4096 is supported; larger
or non-power-of-two requests fail. MVA lookup accepts only recorded ranges,
a live InitMVA handle and nonsecure/noncoherent decoder configuration.
It rejects unknown external VA, rather than pinning arbitrary user pages.

Legacy flush/invalidate optype1 synchronizes the allocation containing the
range; optype0 synchronizes all tracked allocations. Every call performs the
real DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC for streaming imports; coherent memory uses a CPU
barrier. This is not a hardware job fence. Correct cache ownership around
real decode and integration with externally allocated output frames remain
unqualified. Fork children cannot call the inherited bridge or release their
parent's handles. The probe is intended for a private process with controlled
library lifetime, not concurrent plugin unloading in a media service.

Expected success reports four VAL memory kinds and 32 H.264 Create/Release
rounds, with corresponding VAL_ALLOC lines and kernel live counters returning
to zero. This does not call eVDecDrvInit, decode a bitstream, register a codec
with Android, or replace missing /dev/Vcodec controls. See
Documentation/arm/mediatek/mt6580-vcodec-buffers.rst for qualification.
